.custom = { "section": "Declarations and registration" },
---

A PHP stub file (`.stub.php`) declares the extension's namespaces, function signatures, properties, constants and classes. PHP's [stub generator](https://php.github.io/php-src/miscellaneous/stubs.html) reads these declarations and writes a C header.

# [Minimal declaration]($heading.id('minimal-declaration'))

```php
<?php
/**
 * @generate-legacy-arginfo 80200
 * @generate-class-entries
 */
function hello(): void {}
function greet(string $name): string {}
```

`@generate-class-entries` enables registration code generation. The legacy marker requests arginfo compatible with PHP 8.2. Keep the header supplied by the skeleton.

# [Update workflow]($heading.id('update-workflow'))

1. Edit `my_php_extension.stub.php`.
2. Run `zig build gen-stub`.
3. Update the Zig function or method implementations or class bindings.
4. Run `zig build run-tests`.

`zig build gen-stub` and `zig build run-tests` use the respective `gen_stub.php` and `run-tests.php` scripts shipped with phpz.

The generated `*_arginfo.h` typically contains `ext_functions`, function arginfo, `register_class_*` and, when needed, `register_<name>_symbols`. Include `phpz.h` and the arginfo header in the extension header to expose these declarations to Zig's C translation.

Typed functions and methods check required, fixed and variadic parameters, by-reference parameters and direct return reference flags against arginfo at compile time. A mismatch fails the build. Keep PHP types and defaults consistent with runtime parameter parsing and the implementation. For context-only handlers, `expectArgs` checks counts and related metadata in Debug builds. See [Compile-time and Debug declaration checks]($link.page('reference/registration/arguments').ref('debug-declaration-checks')) for the scope and the generic closure exception.

The `@frameless-function` annotation fails at the binding site. phpz does not export `zflf_*` handlers. Remove the annotation and regenerate arginfo.

# [Names and aliases]($heading.id('names-and-aliases'))

`functions`, `namedFunctions` and class method bindings check case-sensitive names and implementation coverage at compile time. Lookup uses a compile-time enum table in stub order and preserves PHP registration order and runtime lookup behavior. Explicit `@alias` declarations reuse existing handlers.

Types and metadata depend on the PHP version. When importing PHP's built-in stubs, use paths the generator can resolve, such as `Zend/zend_attributes.stub.php` in the attribute example. See [Skeleton and code generation]($link.page('getting-started/tools')) for generator updates and documentation.
